Q: Why is this phone number not recognised in iOS Safari? A: <hr />
<p>The issue occurs because Safari's pattern recognition can be inconsistent when phone numbers are mixed with specific keywords like &quot;Bound&quot;. To fix this and ensure 100% reliability, you should take manual control.</p>
<p><strong>1. Disable Auto-Detection</strong> Add this meta tag to your <code>&lt;head&gt;</code> section to stop Safari from guessing:</p>
<p>HTML</p>
<pre><code>&lt;meta name=&quot;format-detection&quot; content=&quot;telephone=no&quot;&gt;
</code></pre>
<p><strong>2. Use Manual <code>tel:</code> Links</strong> Wrap your phone numbers in an anchor tag. This is the standard way to ensure it works across all mobile browsers:</p>
<p>HTML</p>
<pre><code>&lt;p&gt;Bound &lt;a href=&quot;tel:1-555-555-5555&quot;&gt;1-555-555-5555&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
</code></pre>
<p><strong>3. Style for Consistency (CSS)</strong> Use this CSS to prevent the default blue link style while keeping it interactive on mobile:</p>
<p>CSS</p>
<pre><code>a[href^=&quot;tel&quot;] {
    color: inherit;
    text-decoration: none;
}

@media (max-width: 768px) {
    a[href^=&quot;tel&quot;] {
        color: #007bff;
        text-decoration: underline;
    }
}
</code></pre>
<p><strong>Demo:</strong> You can check the fix here <a href="https://codepen.io/aymandevoffical-stack/pen/pvEYRRK" rel="nofollow noreferrer">https://codepen.io/aymandevoffical-stack/pen/pvEYRRK</a></p>

Q: Prevent cntlin to truncate variable values A: <p><strong>'$' is not a valid value for the TYPE variable.</strong> Use 'N' for a numeric format and 'C' for a character format. But really The TYPE variable is not needed since PROC FORMAT will create a character format when the format name starts with a '$'.</p>
<p>Using an invalid value of TYPE causes PROC FORMAT to treat each observation as a NEW request to make the format.  So the result is only the LAST observation actually makes it into the format.</p>
<p>If you want to control the lengths of the format include MIN, MAX and DEFAULT variables in your CNTLIN dataset.
Since you did not include the DEFAULT variable to set the default display width of the format PROC FORMAT will use the maximum of the lengths of the values of LABEL variable. So most likely the last value of <code>thenameofmyvar</code> was only one byte.</p>
<p><strong>But why are you mapping strings like <code>'       1'</code> and <code>'      2'</code> to the values of the variable?</strong></p>
<p>Did you mean to create a format that consists only of the values that appear in the data and just maps those values to themselves?</p>
<p>If so then set the START and LABEL values to the same thing.</p>
<p>So perhaps you want to do something like this.</p>
<pre class="lang-none prettyprint-override"><code>data cntlin;
  set sashelp.class(keep=sex rename=(sex=START));
  retain fmtname &quot;$sex&quot; ;
  label=start ;
run;

proc sort nodupkey;
  by fmtname start;
run;

proc format cntlin=cntlin fmtlib;
run;
</code></pre>
<p>Result</p>
<pre class="lang-none prettyprint-override"><code>----------------------------------------------------------------------------                                               
|       FORMAT NAME: $SEX     LENGTH:    1   NUMBER OF VALUES:    2        |                                                  
|   MIN LENGTH:   1  MAX LENGTH:  40  DEFAULT LENGTH:   1  FUZZ:        0  |                                                  
|--------------------------------------------------------------------------|                                                   
|START           |END             |LABEL  (VER. V7|V8   16APR2026:15:47:21)|                                                  
|----------------+----------------+----------------------------------------|                                                   
|F               |F               |F                                       |                                                  
|M               |M               |M                                       |                                                 
----------------------------------------------------------------------------
</code></pre>
<p>Modify the example code to use the NAME variable instead of the SEX variable to see an example with more and longer values.</p>
